Id: normalization-follows-icu
Title: Normalisation follows the user's ICU
Input: Text that holds a character with a combining class or a
 decomposition that one of the two Unicode versions has and the other
 does not: that of the reference version, Unicode 15.1, and that of
 the ICU library stringi was built with. It is normalised by
 fix_text() and fix_and_explain() (the option normalization, in each
 of the forms NFC, NFD, NFKC and NFKD) and by a normalize step replayed
 with apply_plan(). For example 'a\U00010EFD\u0301', where U+10EFD
 ARABIC SMALL LOW WORD SAKTA was assigned in Unicode 15.0 with the
 combining class 220, and '\U0001E030', MODIFIER LETTER CYRILLIC SMALL
 A, assigned in Unicode 15.0 with a compatibility decomposition.
Reference: The normalisation of Unicode 15.1. Run on ftfy 6.3.1 with
 Python 3.13: fix_and_explain('a\U00010EFD\u0301') returns the text
 '\xe1\U00010efd' with the plan [('normalize', 'NFC')], and
 fix_and_explain('\U0001E030', normalization='NFKC') returns '\u0430'
 with the plan [('normalize', 'NFKC')].
R: The normalisation of the user's ICU, whatever its version; the
 package neither checks the version nor warns. With ICU 71.1, which
 has Unicode 14.0 and does not know these characters, both texts come
 back unchanged with the plan 'none'. With an ICU that has Unicode
 15.0 or later, R gives what the reference version gives. Text whose
 characters were all assigned before both versions is normalised the
 same by both.
Decided: docs/adr/0005-regex-and-unicode-data-are-generated-from-a-reference-python.md
